The attached image, Figure 4 from the npj Vaccines (2017) paper, demonstrates that Nano-11 is actively transported by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) to the draining lymph node. Figure 4a and b show that the percentage of Nano-11-containing myeloid cells in the draining lymph node peaks around day 2 post-injection, indicating dynamic trafficking. Nano-11 is primarily found within migratory dendritic cells (MigDC), followed by monocytes/macrophages (Mo/MΦ) and lymphoid tissue resident DCs (LTDC) (Fig. 4c). The mean fluorescence intensity (MFI) further reveals that MigDCs exhibit significantly higher uptake of Nano-11 per cell compared to other APC subtypes (Fig. 4d), highlighting Nano-11's efficient targeting to these crucial immune cells for vaccine efficacy.